<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diego López V de Haro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1310">1310</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diego López V de Haro was lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a> and one of the leading nobles of the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, a member of the powerful Haro family. In 1300 he granted a town charter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbao">Bilbao</a>, founding the city that became Biscay's chief port. He died in 1310 during the Castilian siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diego-lopez-v-de-haro.jpg" alt="Diego López V de Haro" width="320" height="585" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fernando Pascullo · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Don_Diego_L%C3%B3pez_de_Haro_V.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Diego López was the son of Diego López III de Haro and Constanza de Bearne, and through his paternal grandmother, Urraca Alfonso de León, a great-grandson of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX of León</a>. His brother Lope Díaz III de Haro also held the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a>. Born most likely around 1250, he married the infanta Violant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile">Alfonso X of Castile</a>, in 1282. Their children included Lope Díaz IV de Haro and María Díaz de Haro, who married Juan Núñez II de Lara.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 25 April 1295, after the death of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iv-of-castile">Sancho IV of Castile</a>, he used the instability at the court of the young Ferdinand IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> to take the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a>, which belonged by right to his niece, María Díaz I de Haro; this earned him the nickname el Intruso. That summer the infante John of Castile, her husband, turned the lordship over to him, and in 1300 María renounced her claim in return for other towns. On 15 June 1300 he made the fishing village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbao">Bilbao</a> a town under the authority of the lords of Biscay.</p><p>The infante John soon pressed his wife's claim again. Diego López sided with the infante Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and on Henry's death in 1303 he and Juan Manuel, Prince of Villena, inherited his possessions. In 1304 the king confiscated his lands, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina-del-campo">Medina del Campo</a> in 1305 he arrived with three hundred knights, refused to give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a> and appealed to the pope. After Pope Clement V declared the renunciation of 1300 invalid, an agreement of 1307 let him keep Biscay for life, to pass to María on his death, and the king made him Mayordomo mayor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1309 he joined Ferdinand IV's war against the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. He argued without success for an attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> rather than the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a> that the king chose, and stayed with the king when the infante John and others deserted the camp in October 1309. Late that year he fell ill with gout. He died in the camp at Algeciras in the winter of 1310 and was buried in the Monasterio de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>, where Violant also lay, and María Díaz I de Haro took possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diego López V de Haro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1300 Lord <b>Diego López V de Haro</b> founded Bilbao, which grew into its chief port.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-biscay">Lordship of Biscay</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.13,-5.45&amp;year=1300" aria-label="Algeciras on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Diego López V de Haro"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4624 4468h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4624 4468h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.13,-5.45&amp;year=1300">What was here in 1300? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
